{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>Now Avimelech had not drawn near to her, etc.</b> This may be explained according to what Rambam wrote in chapter 9 of Hilkhot Melakhim: a descendant of Noah who has relations with another man's wife unnaturally is exempt, but with a Jewish woman, even unnaturally, he is liable. Therefore he was cautious out of concern that Sarah's legal status might be that of a Jewish woman's, and he did not draw near to her at all." "<b>Would even a righteous nation, etc.</b> One needs to understand the intent of his saying <b>הֲגוֹי</b> ('would the nation'), and further his saying <b>גַם צַדִּיק</b> ('even righteous'), and further his saying <b>תַּהֲרֹג</b> ('you would kill'), for this is not implied by the words of HaShem, since if the man returns the woman and does not draw near to her, he is not liable to death." "<b>Indeed</b>, it may be explained according to the way the Sages, their memory for a blessing, said (Berakhot 64a) regarding the verse (I Kings 22:17) ״יָשׁוּבוּ אִישׁ לְבֵיתוֹ בְּשָׁלוֹם״ — that they said בְּשָׁלוֹם ('in peace') as one says of the dead, and even though no one died except Ahab king of Israel, the expression of death was applied to the entire people. This demonstrates that the trouble of the king is the trouble of all the people, and it is fitting to hold that harm should not befall the king even though he is held accountable for his deed, by reason of the harm extending to the entirety of the people.\n\nAnd this is what he expresses with astonishment in saying ״הֲגוֹי״ — meaning, he ascribes himself to the status of a nation for the aforementioned reasoning. <b>גַם צַדִּיק</b> — that proportionate to his being righteous, even regarding a single individual, the El would not pervert justice to kill one righteous person.\n\nAnd the reason he said so is that he understood from the words of HaShem that the decree had been sealed upon him to die, as in the saying ״הִנְּךָ מֵת״ ('you shall die') definitively ״עַל הָאִשָּׁה אֲשֶׁר לָקַחְתָּ״ — meaning: you have already taken — for the taking alone.\n\nAnd he argued: is he not righteous, for he is not liable to death for the taking, since he did not draw near to her, even unnaturally, according to the law of Jewish forbidden relations? Behold he is righteous and vindicated in his judgment. And he further added that even if he had had relations with her he would be exempt, for a descendant of Noah who has relations with another man's wife unwittingly is exempt — and he acted unwittingly, as he said: ״הֲלֹא הוּא אָמַר לִי אֲחֹתִי הִיא״." "<b>Or</b> it is possible that he understood from the utterance of HaShem — for He knew that she was a woman who had known a husband — that He said 'it is not so,' ״הֲלֹא הוּא אָמַר לִי״, etc."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 20, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:42:36.045967617Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}